I have a lot of accent macros built into the LaTeX format file I
habitually use.  So char 229, for example, is defined to be
active, and comes out as \=a.  (A-macron is very common in the
texts I write.)

In the tabbing environment, \=, \' and \` are redefined to do
other jobs, and you have to do accents by saying \a=, \a' and \a`.
This is a real pain for people who use accents regularly.

Could the next implementation of LaTeX adopt a principle of not using
the same macro strings to do different jobs in different environments?

Why not, for example, use the ampersand in the tabbing environment, since
we already use it in tabular to do a very similar job?

Best wishes,

Dominik



